
;; ═══ Surface completion 2026-08-02 — the SRFI-125 procedures wired in
;; ═══ lib/std/srfi-125.js that had no reference record. Authored against the
;; ═══ IMPLEMENTATION (every program below was run), with divergences from the
;; ═══ SRFI-125 text recorded in :caveats rather than papered over.

(verb :name "hash-table" :std "SRFI-125" :library "hash" :tier "core" :kind "pure" :signature "(hash-table key1 value1 key2 value2 ...) -> hash-table"
  :summary "Build a hash table in one expression from alternating key/value arguments. Zero arguments gives an empty table; an odd argument count is an error. SRFI-125."
  :explanation "The literal-style constructor. Where make-hash-table gives you an empty table you then fill with hash-table-set!, hash-table lets you write the starting contents inline, which keeps small tables readable at the point of definition. The arguments alternate: key, value, key, value. Later duplicate keys overwrite earlier ones, exactly as repeated hash-table-set! calls would. The returned table is an ordinary mutable table — you can go on setting and deleting in it."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(hash-table-keys (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2))" :note "(a b) — two entries, built inline.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((stats (hash-table 'hp 100 'mp 30 'gold 12)))\n  (hash-table-ref/default stats 'mp 0))" :note "30 — read one field straight out of an inline table.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(let ((squares (apply hash-table (append-map (lambda (i) (list i (* i i))) (iota 5)))))\n  (display (hash-table-values squares))\n  (newline))" :note "(0 1 4 9 16) — flatten a list into alternating key/value arguments, then apply the constructor.")
  )
  :caveats (
    "DIVERGENCE from SRFI-125: the spec's first argument is a COMPARATOR — (hash-table comparator key1 val1 ...). Motoi has no comparator objects (make-hash-table is arity 0), so this constructor omits it entirely and the FIRST argument is already a key. Code copied from another Scheme that passes a comparator will silently treat that comparator as a key."
    "DIVERGENCE from SRFI-125: the spec says the returned table is IMMUTABLE. Motoi has no immutable tables, so the result is an ordinary mutable table and hash-table-set! on it succeeds where a strict implementation would raise."
    "An odd number of arguments is an error: (hash-table 'a) raises \"odd number of arguments (need alternating key/value)\". The pairing is positional, so a single missing value shifts every later key into a value slot."
    "Duplicate keys are allowed and the LAST one wins, matching repeated hash-table-set!. It is not an error, so a typo'd repeat is silent."
    "Do not (display table) directly — a hash table has no printed representation and shows as [object Object]. Display hash-table-keys, hash-table-values, hash-table-size or hash-table->alist instead."
  )
  :usecases ("Small fixed lookup tables written inline — stat blocks, key bindings, colour names." "Test fixtures where the starting contents belong next to the assertion." "Turning a computed flat list of alternating keys and values into a table with apply.")
  :related ("make-hash-table" "alist->hash-table" "hash-table-set!" "hash-table-copy")
  :learn (:concept "Literal construction: state the contents at the point of definition instead of building them up with writes." :prerequisites ("make-hash-table" "hash-table-set!") :progression "Start with make-hash-table plus hash-table-set! so the writes are visible; move to hash-table once the contents are fixed and short; reach for alist->hash-table when the data already arrives as a list of pairs.")
  :provenance "SRFI-125 §Constructors (comparator argument omitted — see :caveats); wired lib/std/srfi-125.js 2026-07-24; reference record 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define key-bindings (hash-table 'up 'move-north 'down 'move-south 'space 'jump))\n(display (list 'space (hash-table-ref/default key-bindings 'space 'unbound)\n               'left (hash-table-ref/default key-bindings 'left 'unbound)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define f (hash-table 0 'zero 1 'one 2 'two))\n(display (list 'domain (hash-table-keys f)\n               'codomain-image (hash-table-values f)\n               'cardinality (hash-table-size f)\n               'total-on-its-domain (equal? 'one (hash-table-ref/default f 1 'UNDEF))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define built-inline (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3))\n(define built-by-writes (make-hash-table))\n(hash-table-set! built-by-writes 'a 1)\n(hash-table-set! built-by-writes 'b 2)\n(hash-table-set! built-by-writes 'c 3)\n(display (list 'same-keys (equal? (hash-table-keys built-inline) (hash-table-keys built-by-writes))\n               'same-values (equal? (hash-table-values built-inline) (hash-table-values built-by-writes))\n               'distinct-objects (not (eq? built-inline built-by-writes))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator is declaring the keyboard map for a scene. The contents are FIXED — three bindings decided at design time, never computed. Three constructors are available. make-hash-table plus three hash-table-set! lines works, but it spreads one decision across four statements and invites a reader to ask whether the writes are conditional. alist->hash-table works, but it forces the operator to type a list of two-element lists, adding a layer of parentheses that carries no information. hash-table states the contents in one expression with one level of nesting. The auditor's question — 'what is in this table?' — is answered by reading a single line, and because the constructor is pure the answer cannot have been altered by an earlier statement."
     :program "(define key-bindings (hash-table 'up 'move-north 'down 'move-south 'space 'jump))\n(display (hash-table-ref/default key-bindings 'space 'unbound))\n(newline)\n(display (hash-table-size key-bindings))\n(newline)"
     :explanation "Line 1 builds the whole table in one expression: three keys, three values, alternating. The reader sees the complete contents without scanning forward for later writes. Lines 2-3 read the space binding and print move-north's sibling, jump. Lines 4-5 print the size, 3, so the auditor can confirm that six arguments became three entries and nothing was silently dropped by an odd-count mistake. The audit tape is one construction and two reads; there is no write after construction, which is what makes the single line trustworthy as the definition of the table's contents."
     :meta "Why hash-table and not make-hash-table plus writes: the writes form would let a later line change a binding without the reader noticing, so the auditor would have to read the whole file to answer 'what is bound to space?'. With the inline constructor the answer is local. Why not alist->hash-table: the alist form adds a wrapping list per pair, and the operator does not have an alist — the operator has six literal values. Converting them to an alist first is work invented to satisfy a constructor, not work the problem asked for.")
    (:eng "Inline construction is a readability decision, not a performance one. Use it when the contents are short, fixed, and belong next to the name; switch to make-hash-table plus writes as soon as any entry is conditional, because a conditional key inside an argument list is far harder to read than an if around a set!."
     :math "The constructor takes a finite sequence (k1, v1, ..., kn, vn) and returns the partial function f: K ⇀ V with dom(f) = {k1, ..., kn} and f(ki) = vi, resolving duplicates by taking the last occurrence. The zero-argument case returns the empty partial function ∅."
     :cs "This is the map-literal idiom: Python's {a: 1, b: 2}, JavaScript's object literal, Clojure's {:a 1}. Motoi spells it as a variadic procedure rather than reader syntax, which has one real consequence — the arguments are ordinary expressions, so keys and values may be computed, and the whole thing composes with apply.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Declaring a finite mapping all at once: instead of creating an empty container and filling it, name every pair in a single expression so the contents and the name arrive together."
     :rungs ((:domain "hash" :code "(define key-bindings (hash-table 'up 'move-north 'down 'move-south 'space 'jump))\n(display (hash-table-ref/default key-bindings 'space 'unbound))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "a filled spice rack, labelled before it leaves the shop" :code "(define rack (hash-table 'cumin 'jar-1 'paprika 'jar-2 'salt 'jar-3))\n(display (list 'labels (hash-table-keys rack) 'jars (hash-table-values rack)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "a function defined by its table of values" :code "(define (tabulate . kv) (apply hash-table kv))\n(define f (tabulate 0 'zero 1 'one 2 'two))\n(display (map (lambda (x) (hash-table-ref/default f x 'undefined)) (list 0 1 2 3)))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "In the hash-table domain the constructor takes alternating arguments and returns a populated table; nothing is added afterwards. In the spice-rack domain the rack arrives with its jars already in their slots and the labels already written, so there is no moment in which the rack exists but is unlabelled. In the function domain a finite function can be given by exhibiting its table — f(0) = zero, f(1) = one, f(2) = two — with no rule and no construction process, and asking for f(3) yields undefined because 3 was never in the table. All three share the shape: a mapping comes into existence complete, and its extension is exactly what was written down."
     :meta "The shape transfers because 'defined by extension' is a general way to specify a mapping — you list the pairs rather than give a rule for computing them. Anything with a finite, known, unchanging set of associations borrows it: an enum-to-string table, a routing table typed by hand, a chemical symbol table, a set of default settings shipped with a program."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define t (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3))\n(display (list 'size (hash-table-size t)\n               'a (hash-table-ref/default t 'a 'MISS)\n               'b (hash-table-ref/default t 'b 'MISS)\n               'c (hash-table-ref/default t 'c 'MISS)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (extension-law ks vs)\n  (let ((t (apply hash-table (append-map (lambda (k v) (list k v)) ks vs))))\n    (and (= (hash-table-size t) (length ks))\n         (equal? (hash-table-keys t) ks)\n         (equal? (hash-table-values t) vs))))\n(display (list 'three (extension-law (list 'a 'b 'c) (list 1 2 3))\n               'one (extension-law (list 'solo) (list 9))\n               'empty (= 0 (hash-table-size (hash-table)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (agrees-with-writes? kvs)\n  (let ((inline (apply hash-table kvs))\n        (written (make-hash-table)))\n    (let loop ((rest kvs))\n      (if (null? rest)\n          (and (equal? (hash-table-keys inline) (hash-table-keys written))\n               (equal? (hash-table-values inline) (hash-table-values written)))\n          (begin (hash-table-set! written (car rest) (cadr rest))\n                 (loop (cddr rest)))))))\n(display (list 'plain (agrees-with-writes? (list 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3))\n               'with-duplicate (agrees-with-writes? (list 'a 1 'b 2 'a 99))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "(hash-table k1 v1 ... kn vn) is observationally identical to make-hash-table followed by hash-table-set! of each pair in order: same keys in the same order, same values, last duplicate winning."
     :program "(define inline (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'a 99))\n(define written (make-hash-table))\n(hash-table-set! written 'a 1)\n(hash-table-set! written 'b 2)\n(hash-table-set! written 'a 99)\n(display (list 'keys-eq (equal? (hash-table-keys inline) (hash-table-keys written))\n               'values-eq (equal? (hash-table-values inline) (hash-table-values written))\n               'last-wins (equal? 99 (hash-table-ref/default inline 'a 'MISS))))\n(newline)"
     :invariant "∀ k1 v1 ... kn vn. hash-table(k1,v1,...,kn,vn) ≡ fold over i of set!(make-hash-table(), ki, vi) ∧ size = |{k1..kn}| ∧ ∀ i<j with ki = kj. ref(result, ki) = vj"
     :meta "The pattern is constructor-equals-replay: a bulk constructor is correct exactly when it is indistinguishable from replaying the primitive writes in argument order. What would falsify it: a different key ordering, a duplicate resolved to the earlier value, or a size that counted argument pairs instead of distinct keys. Note the duplicate case is the sharp one — it is where a naive 'count the pairs' implementation would disagree. See book-of-code ch08 on write ordering."
     :eng "The engineering payoff is substitutability: any inline table can be rewritten as explicit writes during debugging (to add a display between them) and back again, with no behavioural change to worry about."
     :math "The claim identifies the extensional definition of a finite partial function with the iterated point-update ∅[k1 ↦ v1]...[kn ↦ vn]. Duplicate keys are absorbed because point-update is idempotent in its key and last-writer in its value."
     :cs "This is the standard bulk-constructor contract, the same one that makes Python's dict(pairs) agree with a loop of __setitem__ calls. Implementations that batch-insert must preserve the sequential semantics for duplicates, which is the only place batching can go wrong.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define defaults (hash-table 'volume 8 'difficulty 'normal 'subtitles #f))\n(define (with-overrides base overrides)\n  (let ((merged (hash-table-copy base)))\n    (hash-table-for-each (lambda (k v) (hash-table-set! merged k v)) overrides)\n    merged))\n(define settings (with-overrides defaults (hash-table 'difficulty 'hard)))\n(display (list 'volume (hash-table-ref/default settings 'volume 'none)\n               'difficulty (hash-table-ref/default settings 'difficulty 'none)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (compose-tables g f)\n  (let ((out (make-hash-table)))\n    (hash-table-for-each\n      (lambda (k v) (if (hash-table-contains? g v)\n                        (hash-table-set! out k (hash-table-ref g v (lambda () 'undefined)))))\n      f)\n    out))\n(define f (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3))\n(define g (hash-table 1 'one 2 'two))\n(define gf (compose-tables g f))\n(display (list 'domain (hash-table-keys gf) 'image (hash-table-values gf)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define opcodes (hash-table 'nop 0 'push 1 'pop 2 'add 3))\n(define (assemble program)\n  (map (lambda (op) (hash-table-ref opcodes op (lambda () (error 'unknown-opcode)))) program))\n(display (assemble (list 'push 'push 'add 'pop)))\n(newline)"
     :base "hash-table builds a populated table from alternating key/value arguments in one expression."
     :composition "(define defaults (hash-table 'volume 8 'difficulty 'normal 'subtitles #f))\n(define (with-overrides base overrides)\n  (let ((merged (hash-table-copy base)))\n    (hash-table-for-each (lambda (k v) (hash-table-set! merged k v)) overrides)\n    merged))\n(define settings (with-overrides defaults (hash-table 'difficulty 'hard)))\n(display (hash-table-ref/default settings 'volume 'none))\n(newline)\n(display (hash-table-ref/default settings 'difficulty 'none))\n(newline)"
     :emergent "declarative override layer — both the base and the patch are written as literal tables, so a settings change is a one-line diff of data rather than an edit to control flow"
     :meta "The composition chains hash-table (two literal tables) with hash-table-copy (don't mutate the base) and hash-table-for-each plus hash-table-set! (apply the patch). What emerges is the layered-declaration family: because construction is an expression, a configuration LAYER is itself a value that can be passed, stored, and diffed. The same family appears as Kubernetes overlays, as Nix attribute-set merging, and as CSS cascade layers. The distinguishing mark against plain construction is that no layer names its own position — the merge order decides, so layers stay reusable. See book-of-composition ch12 on layered configuration.")
)
)

(verb :name "hash-table-copy" :std "SRFI-125" :library "hash" :tier "core" :kind "pure" :signature "(hash-table-copy h [mutable]) -> hash-table"
  :summary "A new hash table holding the same associations as h. Writing to the copy does not touch the original. The optional mutable argument is accepted and ignored. SRFI-125."
  :explanation "The snapshot verb. Hash tables are mutable and are passed by reference, so handing one to another part of the program hands over the power to change it. hash-table-copy breaks that link: the result starts out with exactly the same keys and values, but the two tables are separate objects from then on. The copy is SHALLOW — the keys and the values themselves are the very same objects, so a mutable value stored in both tables is still shared. Motoi's copy also preserves iteration order."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(hash-table-size (hash-table-copy (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2)))" :note "2 — same number of entries as the original.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let* ((original (hash-table 'hp 100))\n       (backup (hash-table-copy original)))\n  (hash-table-set! original 'hp 5)\n  (list (hash-table-ref/default original 'hp 0)\n        (hash-table-ref/default backup 'hp 0)))" :note "(5 100) — the backup kept the old value; the two tables are independent.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(let* ((base (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3))\n       (probe (hash-table-copy base)))\n  (hash-table-prune! (lambda (k v) (odd? v)) probe)\n  (display (list 'probe (hash-table-keys probe) 'base-intact (hash-table-keys base)))\n  (newline))" :note "(probe (b) base-intact (a b c)) — copy first, then destroy the copy, and the original survives a mutating verb.")
  )
  :caveats (
    "DIVERGENCE from SRFI-125: the spec says (hash-table-copy h #f) returns an IMMUTABLE table. Motoi has no immutable tables — the second argument is accepted and completely ignored, and the result is always mutable. Do not rely on the copy being write-protected."
    "The copy is SHALLOW. A list or table stored as a value lives in both tables at once, so mutating that value is visible through either. Only the key-to-value bindings are duplicated."
    "Unlike the newer SRFI-125 verbs in this library, hash-table-copy is LENIENT with a non-table: (hash-table-copy 5) returns an empty table rather than raising. That silence can hide an argument-order mistake."
    "Copying an n-entry table is O(n) in time and space. Copying inside a loop over the same table turns a linear pass into a quadratic one."
  )
  :usecases ("Snapshotting state before a risky or destructive operation so it can be restored." "Handing a table to code you do not want to grant write access to your own." "Building a modified version of a table without disturbing the caller's copy — the copy-then-mutate idiom.")
  :related ("hash-table" "make-hash-table" "hash-table->alist" "hash-table-map" "hash-table-prune!")
  :learn (:concept "Value semantics recovered from reference semantics by explicit copying." :prerequisites ("make-hash-table" "hash-table-set!") :progression "Understand first that passing a table shares it; then use hash-table-copy to opt out of sharing; then learn where the sharing survives anyway (the shallow-copy caveat).")
  :provenance "SRFI-125 §Copying (mutable argument ignored — see :caveats); wired lib/std/srfi-125.js 2026-07-24; reference record 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define checkpoint (hash-table 'level 3 'score 1200 'lives 2))\n(define live (hash-table-copy checkpoint))\n(hash-table-set! live 'lives 1)\n(hash-table-set! live 'score 1500)\n(display (list 'live-lives (hash-table-ref/default live 'lives 0)\n               'checkpoint-lives (hash-table-ref/default checkpoint 'lives 0)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define f (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3))\n(define g (hash-table-copy f))\n(display (list 'same-domain (equal? (hash-table-keys f) (hash-table-keys g))\n               'same-image (equal? (hash-table-values f) (hash-table-values g))\n               'agree-pointwise (equal? (map (lambda (k) (hash-table-ref/default f k 'U)) (hash-table-keys f))\n                                        (map (lambda (k) (hash-table-ref/default g k 'U)) (hash-table-keys f)))\n               'not-identical (not (eq? f g))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define shared-list (list 1 2 3))\n(define a (hash-table 'payload shared-list 'n 7))\n(define b (hash-table-copy a))\n(hash-table-set! b 'n 99)\n(display (list 'binding-copied (list (hash-table-ref/default a 'n 0) (hash-table-ref/default b 'n 0))\n               'value-still-shared (eq? (hash-table-ref/default a 'payload #f)\n                                        (hash-table-ref/default b 'payload #f))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator is about to run a speculative simulation step that writes all over the entity table, and needs to be able to roll back if the step is rejected. Three approaches are on the table. Journalling — record every write and undo them in reverse — is precise but requires wrapping every mutating call site, and one unwrapped call silently breaks the rollback. Rebuilding from the alist via hash-table->alist and alist->hash-table works and is only slightly longer, but it goes through an intermediate representation that will drop anything the alist form cannot carry, so the auditor has to check the round trip. hash-table-copy takes the snapshot in one call with no intermediate form, and the rollback is simply keeping the old reference. The audit question 'can this be undone?' is answered by pointing at one line."
     :program "(define checkpoint (hash-table 'level 3 'score 1200 'lives 2))\n(define live (hash-table-copy checkpoint))\n(hash-table-set! live 'lives 1)\n(display (hash-table-ref/default live 'lives 0))\n(newline)\n(display (hash-table-ref/default checkpoint 'lives 0))\n(newline)"
     :explanation "Line 1 builds the checkpoint — the state the operator is willing to return to. Line 2 takes the working copy; from this point the two names denote two objects, and that is the whole safety property. Line 3 spends a life in the working copy. Lines 4-5 print the working value, 1. Lines 6-7 print the checkpoint value, still 2. The audit tape shows one copy, one write, and two reads that disagree — and the disagreement is the evidence that the copy did its job. If lines 4-7 printed the same number, the copy would have been an alias and the rollback would be an illusion."
     :meta "Why hash-table-copy and not journalling: journalling distributes the correctness obligation over every call site, so an auditor must inspect all of them; the copy concentrates it in one line. Why not the alist round trip: that path asserts two conversions are inverse, so the auditor now has to trust hash-table->alist and alist->hash-table as well as the intent. One verb, one obligation.")
    (:eng "Take the copy at the boundary — right before you hand a table out, or right before a destructive pass — not deep inside a loop. The cost is linear in the entry count, and a copy taken in the wrong place is both slower and less obviously the snapshot."
     :math "The copy is the identity map on a finite partial function's graph: given f with graph {(k, f(k)) : k ∈ dom(f)}, it returns g with the same graph. f and g are equal as functions and distinct as objects, which is exactly the difference between extensional equality and identity."
     :cs "This is shallow clone. Related: Python's dict.copy, Java's HashMap copy constructor, JavaScript's Object.assign({}, o). All share the same trap — one level of structure is duplicated, everything below it stays shared, and the bug shows up only when a nested value is mutated.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Taking a snapshot: produce a second, independent thing that currently agrees with the first everywhere, so that changes to one no longer show up in the other."
     :rungs ((:domain "hash" :code "(define checkpoint (hash-table 'level 3 'lives 2))\n(define live (hash-table-copy checkpoint))\n(hash-table-set! live 'lives 1)\n(display (list (hash-table-ref/default live 'lives 0) (hash-table-ref/default checkpoint 'lives 0)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "a photocopy of a form" :code "(define original-form (hash-table 'name 'unfilled 'date 'unfilled))\n(define draft (hash-table-copy original-form))\n(hash-table-set! draft 'name 'scribbled-over)\n(display (list 'draft (hash-table-ref/default draft 'name 'blank)\n               'original (hash-table-ref/default original-form 'name 'blank)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "two names for equal functions" :code "(define f (hash-table 1 'a 2 'b))\n(define g (hash-table-copy f))\n(define (agree-everywhere? p q ks)\n  (let loop ((ks ks))\n    (cond ((null? ks) #t)\n          ((equal? (hash-table-ref/default p (car ks) 'U) (hash-table-ref/default q (car ks) 'U)) (loop (cdr ks)))\n          (else #f))))\n(display (list 'extensionally-equal (agree-everywhere? f g (hash-table-keys f)) 'identical (eq? f g)))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "In the hash-table domain the copy is a fresh table that starts out agreeing with the original and then diverges under mutation. On paper, a photocopy of a blank form agrees with the original in every mark it carries, and scribbling on the copy leaves the original clean — the copy is what makes it safe to experiment. In the function domain, f and g agree at every point of the domain yet are not the same object, which is the standard distinction between two functions being EQUAL and being IDENTICAL. All three share the shape: agreement now, independence later."
     :meta "The shape transfers to anything with a distinction between what a thing IS and what it CONTAINS: a version-control branch, a copy-on-write filesystem clone, a database snapshot, a git stash. Wherever the two notions come apart, copying is the operation that converts sharing into agreement, and the follow-on question is always the same — how deep does the copy go?"))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define src (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2))\n(define dst (hash-table-copy src))\n(hash-table-set! dst 'c 3)\n(hash-table-delete! dst 'a)\n(display (list 'src-keys (hash-table-keys src) 'dst-keys (hash-table-keys dst)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (copy-agrees? kvs)\n  (let* ((f (apply hash-table kvs))\n         (g (hash-table-copy f)))\n    (and (= (hash-table-size f) (hash-table-size g))\n         (equal? (hash-table-keys f) (hash-table-keys g))\n         (equal? (hash-table-values f) (hash-table-values g))\n         (not (eq? f g)))))\n(display (list 'three (copy-agrees? (list 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3))\n               'one (copy-agrees? (list 'solo 9))\n               'empty (copy-agrees? (list))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (isolation-witness)\n  (let* ((src (hash-table 'k 'ORIGINAL))\n         (dst (hash-table-copy src)))\n    (hash-table-set! dst 'k 'CHANGED)\n    (hash-table-set! dst 'extra 'ADDED)\n    (list 'src-unchanged (equal? 'ORIGINAL (hash-table-ref/default src 'k 'MISS))\n          'src-size-unchanged (= 1 (hash-table-size src))\n          'dst-changed (equal? 'CHANGED (hash-table-ref/default dst 'k 'MISS)))))\n(display (isolation-witness))\n(newline)"
     :claim "hash-table-copy returns a table that agrees with the original on every key at the moment of copying, is not the same object, and is unaffected by every subsequent mutation of either table."
     :program "(define src (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2))\n(define dst (hash-table-copy src))\n(display (list 'agree-at-copy (and (equal? (hash-table-keys src) (hash-table-keys dst))\n                                   (equal? (hash-table-values src) (hash-table-values dst)))))\n(newline)\n(hash-table-set! dst 'a 99)\n(hash-table-set! src 'b 77)\n(display (list 'src-a (hash-table-ref/default src 'a 'MISS)\n               'dst-a (hash-table-ref/default dst 'a 'MISS)\n               'src-b (hash-table-ref/default src 'b 'MISS)\n               'dst-b (hash-table-ref/default dst 'b 'MISS)))\n(newline)"
     :invariant "∀ h. let c = copy(h) in (∀ k. ref(c, k) = ref(h, k)) ∧ ¬eq?(c, h) ∧ ∀ mutation m. m(c) ⇏ observable(h) ∧ m(h) ⇏ observable(c)"
     :meta "The pattern is snapshot-independence, and it has two halves that must both be checked: agreement at time zero, and non-interference afterwards. The second half is the one that fails in practice, because an implementation that returns the same object passes the first half perfectly. What would falsify: eq? returning true for the pair, a write to the copy showing up in the original, or a key present in one and absent in the other immediately after the copy. Note that the invariant is about BINDINGS — it says nothing about mutable values stored inside, which is the shallow-copy caveat. See book-of-code ch11 on aliasing."
     :eng "The engineering payoff is that a copied table can be handed to untrusted or experimental code without an audit of what that code does to it. The trust boundary is the copy call."
     :math "The claim is that copy is a section of the forgetful map from objects to their graphs: it picks, for a given graph, a new object with that graph. Agreement is graph equality; independence is the statement that the two objects have separate mutable state."
     :cs "This is the defensive-copy contract from API design. The classic failure is returning an internal collection directly, which lets a caller mutate your state; the classic over-correction is copying on every access, which turns O(1) reads into O(n).")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (try-move! world dx dy)\n  (let ((candidate (hash-table-copy world)))\n    (hash-table-set! candidate 'x (+ (hash-table-ref/default world 'x 0) dx))\n    (hash-table-set! candidate 'y (+ (hash-table-ref/default world 'y 0) dy))\n    (if (and (>= (hash-table-ref/default candidate 'x 0) 0)\n             (>= (hash-table-ref/default candidate 'y 0) 0))\n        candidate\n        world)))\n(define w (hash-table 'x 2 'y 2))\n(define ok (try-move! w 1 1))\n(define blocked (try-move! w -5 0))\n(display (list 'accepted (list (hash-table-ref/default ok 'x 0) (hash-table-ref/default ok 'y 0))\n               'rejected-kept-old (list (hash-table-ref/default blocked 'x 0) (hash-table-ref/default blocked 'y 0))))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (restrict h keep?)\n  (let ((out (hash-table-copy h)))\n    (hash-table-prune! (lambda (k v) (not (keep? k v))) out)\n    out))\n(define f (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3 'd 4))\n(define evens (restrict f (lambda (k v) (even? v))))\n(display (list 'restricted-domain (hash-table-keys evens)\n               'original-domain-intact (hash-table-keys f)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (versions-of base patches)\n  (map (lambda (patch)\n         (let ((v (hash-table-copy base)))\n           (hash-table-for-each (lambda (k val) (hash-table-set! v k val)) patch)\n           v))\n       patches))\n(define base (hash-table 'mode 'normal 'speed 1))\n(define vs (versions-of base (list (hash-table 'mode 'fast) (hash-table 'speed 3))))\n(display (map (lambda (v) (list (hash-table-ref/default v 'mode 'none) (hash-table-ref/default v 'speed 0))) vs))\n(newline)\n(display (list 'base-untouched (hash-table-ref/default base 'mode 'none)))\n(newline)"
     :base "hash-table-copy returns an independent table with the same associations."
     :composition "(define (try-move! world dx dy)\n  (let ((candidate (hash-table-copy world)))\n    (hash-table-set! candidate 'x (+ (hash-table-ref/default world 'x 0) dx))\n    (hash-table-set! candidate 'y (+ (hash-table-ref/default world 'y 0) dy))\n    (if (and (>= (hash-table-ref/default candidate 'x 0) 0)\n             (>= (hash-table-ref/default candidate 'y 0) 0))\n        candidate\n        world)))\n(define w (hash-table 'x 2 'y 2))\n(display (list (hash-table-ref/default (try-move! w 1 1) 'x 0)\n               (hash-table-ref/default (try-move! w -5 0) 'x 0)))\n(newline)"
     :emergent "speculative execution with rollback — mutate a copy freely, then either publish it or discard it, so the expensive validity check happens on a real candidate state instead of on a prediction of one"
     :meta "The composition chains hash-table-copy (fork the state) with hash-table-set! (apply the tentative change) and a predicate (accept or discard). What emerges is the transaction family: because the mutations land in a throwaway object, correctness no longer depends on being able to undo them. The same family appears as database transactions with rollback, as React's immutable-state updates, as copy-on-write branches in a filesystem, and as the try-move idiom in every collision-detection routine. It is more than a copy call because the copy is what makes the ALL-OR-NOTHING property possible — without it, a partially applied move would leave the world in a state that never should have existed. See book-of-composition ch11 on transactional update.")
)
)

(verb :name "hash-table-count" :std "SRFI-125" :library "hash" :tier "core" :kind "pure" :signature "(hash-table-count h pred) -> integer"
  :summary "How many associations in h satisfy pred, which is called as (pred key value). Table first, predicate second. SRFI-125."
  :explanation "hash-table-size answers 'how many entries?'. hash-table-count answers the narrower question 'how many entries LIKE THIS?'. The predicate receives both the key and the value, so the condition can be about either or both. Every association is visited, so the cost is linear — this is a scan, not an index lookup. The predicate's result is read with ordinary Scheme truth: anything other than #f counts."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(hash-table-count (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3) (lambda (k v) (> v 1)))" :note "2 — two values exceed 1.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((entities (hash-table 'player 'alive 'goblin 'dead 'bat 'alive)))\n  (hash-table-count entities (lambda (k v) (eq? v 'alive))))" :note "2 — the predicate can ignore the key entirely and look only at the value.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(let ((scores (hash-table 'ann 90 'bob 55 'cy 72 'dee 40)))\n  (display (list 'passing (hash-table-count scores (lambda (k v) (>= v 60)))\n                 'failing (hash-table-count scores (lambda (k v) (< v 60)))\n                 'total (hash-table-size scores)))\n  (newline))" :note "(passing 2 failing 2 total 4) — a predicate and its negation partition the table, so the two counts always sum to the size.")
  )
  :caveats (
    "The argument order is TABLE FIRST. Its siblings hash-table-find, hash-table-prune! and hash-table-map take the procedure first. SRFI-125's ordering is not uniform and Motoi follows the spec rather than tidying it, so check the signature before writing the call."
    "The predicate takes TWO arguments, (pred key value), not one. Passing a one-argument predicate such as even? is an arity error, not a silent success."
    "Truthiness is Scheme truthiness: only #f fails to count. A predicate that returns 0 or the empty list counts the entry, because those are true values in Scheme."
    "Cost is O(n) — every association is visited even if the answer becomes certain early. There is no short circuit, so this is not the verb for 'is there at least one?'; use hash-table-find for that."
    "Passing a non-table raises with a message that names the expected argument order rather than returning 0."
  )
  :usecases ("Reporting on a table — how many entities are alive, how many settings are non-default, how many cache slots are stale." "Sanity assertions in tests: the count of entries matching an invariant should equal the size." "Partitioning statistics, by counting a predicate and its negation.")
  :related ("hash-table-size" "hash-table-find" "hash-table-fold" "hash-table-prune!")
  :learn (:concept "Filtered cardinality — the size of a subset picked out by a predicate." :prerequisites ("hash-table-size" "lambda") :progression "Learn hash-table-size for the unconditional count; then hash-table-count for the conditional one; then hash-table-fold when you need something other than a count out of the same scan.")
  :provenance "SRFI-125 §The whole hash table; wired lib/std/srfi-125.js 2026-08-02; reference record 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define entities (hash-table 'player 'alive 'goblin 'dead 'bat 'alive 'slime 'dead))\n(display (list 'alive (hash-table-count entities (lambda (k v) (eq? v 'alive)))\n               'dead (hash-table-count entities (lambda (k v) (eq? v 'dead)))\n               'total (hash-table-size entities)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define f (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3 'd 4 'e 5))\n(define p (lambda (k v) (even? v)))\n(define not-p (lambda (k v) (not (even? v))))\n(display (list 'satisfying (hash-table-count f p)\n               'complement (hash-table-count f not-p)\n               'partition-holds (= (hash-table-size f)\n                                   (+ (hash-table-count f p) (hash-table-count f not-p)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define cache (hash-table 'a 'fresh 'b 'stale 'c 'fresh 'd 'stale 'e 'stale))\n(define stale (hash-table-count cache (lambda (k v) (eq? v 'stale))))\n(define total (hash-table-size cache))\n(display (list 'stale stale 'total total\n               'evict-now (> (* 2 stale) total)))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator needs a health number for the entity table: what fraction of entities are still alive, reported once per frame. Three routes exist. hash-table-fold with an accumulator that adds 1 on a match works and is fully general, but it makes the reader decode an accumulator to discover that the answer is a count. hash-table-keys followed by filter and length works, but it allocates a list of every key and then a second list of matching keys, so a read-only question costs two allocations proportional to the table. hash-table-count says the intent in the verb's name and allocates nothing. The auditor reading 'count' knows the result is a non-negative integer no larger than the size, and can check the call by reading one predicate."
     :program "(define entities (hash-table 'player 'alive 'goblin 'dead 'bat 'alive 'slime 'dead))\n(display (hash-table-count entities (lambda (k v) (eq? v 'alive))))\n(newline)\n(display (hash-table-size entities))\n(newline)"
     :explanation "Line 1 builds the entity table with four entries, two alive and two dead. Lines 2-3 count the alive ones by passing a predicate that ignores the key and tests the value with eq?; the answer is 2. Lines 4-5 print the total size, 4, so the auditor can see the count against its ceiling. The audit tape is one construction and two read-only queries, and the second query is what makes the first interpretable — a count of 2 means nothing until you know whether the table holds four entities or four hundred."
     :meta "Why hash-table-count and not hash-table-fold: fold is the general scan and a count is one of its specialisations, so writing the fold obliges the auditor to verify that the accumulator really is counting — that it starts at 0, adds exactly 1, and never adds on a miss. The named verb discharges all three obligations by construction. Why not keys-then-filter-then-length: that path allocates two intermediate lists and lets the reader wonder whether the filter and the length agree about what a match is.")
    (:eng "Report a count alongside the size it is drawn from. A bare count is not actionable — 12 stale entries is a crisis in a table of 12 and a rounding error in a table of 12,000."
     :math "For a finite partial function f and a predicate P over pairs, the result is |{k ∈ dom(f) : P(k, f(k))}| — the cardinality of the subset of the graph cut out by P. Because P and ¬P partition the graph, count(f, P) + count(f, ¬P) = |dom(f)| always."
     :cs "This is a full linear scan with a counter — the COUNT(*) ... WHERE of the hash-table interface. Related: Python's sum(1 for k, v in d.items() if p(k, v)), Java's stream filter-count, SQL's conditional aggregate. None of them can beat O(n) without a secondary index, which is exactly why databases build one.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Counting by a condition: walk a whole collection, test each member against a yes-or-no question, and report how many said yes — without keeping the members themselves."
     :rungs ((:domain "hash" :code "(define entities (hash-table 'player 'alive 'goblin 'dead 'bat 'alive))\n(display (hash-table-count entities (lambda (k v) (eq? v 'alive))))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "a headcount at the door" :code "(define guests (hash-table 'ann 'checked-in 'bob 'no-show 'cy 'checked-in 'dee 'checked-in))\n(display (list 'in-the-room (hash-table-count guests (lambda (name status) (eq? status 'checked-in)))\n               'invited (hash-table-size guests)))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "cardinality of a defined subset" :code "(define f (hash-table 1 10 2 20 3 30 4 40))\n(define (card-of-subset p) (hash-table-count f p))\n(display (list 'even-keys (card-of-subset (lambda (k v) (even? k)))\n               'values-over-15 (card-of-subset (lambda (k v) (> v 15)))\n               'whole-set (card-of-subset (lambda (k v) #t))\n               'empty-set (card-of-subset (lambda (k v) #f))))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "In the hash-table domain the count is a scan with a two-argument predicate over key and value. At a door, the greeter walks the guest list, asks one question per name, and reports a number — the identities are consulted but not retained, which is exactly why a headcount is faster to report than a guest list. In the set-theoretic domain, a predicate defines a subset by comprehension and the count is that subset's cardinality; the two degenerate predicates are informative, since the always-true one recovers the size and the always-false one gives 0. All three share the shape: a predicate turns a collection into a number, and the number forgets everything except how many."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a collection can be enumerated and each member can be judged independently — a poll, a census, a linter counting violations, a test runner counting failures. The independence is the load-bearing part: because no member's judgement depends on another's, the scan may be reordered or split across workers without changing the answer."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define t (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3 'd 4))\n(display (list 'all (hash-table-count t (lambda (k v) #t))\n               'none (hash-table-count t (lambda (k v) #f))\n               'size (hash-table-size t)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (partition-law t p)\n  (= (hash-table-size t)\n     (+ (hash-table-count t p)\n        (hash-table-count t (lambda (k v) (not (p k v)))))))\n(define t (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3 'd 4 'e 5))\n(display (list 'even-values (partition-law t (lambda (k v) (even? v)))\n               'big-values (partition-law t (lambda (k v) (> v 3)))\n               'always (partition-law t (lambda (k v) #t))\n               'never (partition-law t (lambda (k v) #f))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define t (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3 'd 4 'e 5 'f 6))\n(define (count-via-fold p)\n  (hash-table-fold t (lambda (k v acc) (if (p k v) (+ acc 1) acc)) 0))\n(define (agrees? p) (= (hash-table-count t p) (count-via-fold p)))\n(display (list 'even (agrees? (lambda (k v) (even? v)))\n               'over-3 (agrees? (lambda (k v) (> v 3)))\n               'all (agrees? (lambda (k v) #t))\n               'none (agrees? (lambda (k v) #f))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "hash-table-count is bounded by the size, returns the size for an always-true predicate and 0 for an always-false one, and a predicate together with its negation always partitions the table: the two counts sum to the size."
     :program "(define t (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3 'd 4 'e 5))\n(define p (lambda (k v) (even? v)))\n(define hits (hash-table-count t p))\n(define misses (hash-table-count t (lambda (k v) (not (p k v)))))\n(display (list 'hits hits 'misses misses 'size (hash-table-size t)))\n(newline)\n(display (list 'partition (= (+ hits misses) (hash-table-size t))\n               'bounded (and (<= 0 hits) (<= hits (hash-table-size t)))\n               'all (= (hash-table-size t) (hash-table-count t (lambda (k v) #t)))\n               'none (= 0 (hash-table-count t (lambda (k v) #f)))))\n(newline)"
     :invariant "∀ h P. 0 ≤ count(h, P) ≤ size(h) ∧ count(h, λ.#t) = size(h) ∧ count(h, λ.#f) = 0 ∧ count(h, P) + count(h, ¬P) = size(h)"
     :meta "The pattern is predicate-partition-conservation, and it is the strongest cheap check available for any counting operation: it is one equation, it holds for every predicate, and it fails loudly if the scan skips an entry or visits one twice. What would falsify: a count exceeding the size (double visiting), the two counts summing to less than the size (a skipped entry), or the always-true predicate disagreeing with hash-table-size (a broken traversal). See book-of-math ch03 on partitions and book-of-code ch09 on traversal completeness."
     :eng "The engineering payoff is a free assertion: any place you compute a count and its complement, checking that they sum to the size costs one comparison and detects a whole class of traversal bugs."
     :math "The claim is the additivity of counting measure over a partition. A predicate P splits the graph of f into P-true and P-false parts, which are disjoint and cover, so their cardinalities add to |dom(f)|. The two degenerate predicates give the boundary values 0 and |dom(f)|."
     :cs "This is the completeness contract for a linear scan. It is the same property tested by counting rows in a table with and without a WHERE clause; a mismatch indicates the traversal, not the predicate, is at fault.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (health-report entities)\n  (let ((total (hash-table-size entities)))\n    (list 'alive (hash-table-count entities (lambda (k v) (eq? v 'alive)))\n          'dead (hash-table-count entities (lambda (k v) (eq? v 'dead)))\n          'total total\n          'wipe (= 0 (hash-table-count entities (lambda (k v) (eq? v 'alive)))))))\n(define e (hash-table 'player 'alive 'goblin 'dead 'bat 'dead))\n(display (health-report e))\n(newline)\n(hash-table-set! e 'player 'dead)\n(display (health-report e))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (histogram t buckets)\n  (map (lambda (bucket)\n         (list (car bucket) (hash-table-count t (cadr bucket))))\n       buckets))\n(define scores (hash-table 'a 95 'b 82 'c 71 'd 65 'e 45 'f 88))\n(display (histogram scores\n           (list (list 'high (lambda (k v) (>= v 85)))\n                 (list 'mid (lambda (k v) (and (>= v 60) (< v 85))))\n                 (list 'low (lambda (k v) (< v 60))))))\n(newline)\n(display (list 'sums-to-size (= (hash-table-size scores)\n                                (+ (hash-table-count scores (lambda (k v) (>= v 85)))\n                                   (hash-table-count scores (lambda (k v) (and (>= v 60) (< v 85))))\n                                   (hash-table-count scores (lambda (k v) (< v 60)))))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (load-factor-alarm cache threshold)\n  (let* ((n (hash-table-size cache))\n         (stale (hash-table-count cache (lambda (k v) (eq? v 'stale)))))\n    (cond ((= n 0) 'empty)\n          ((> (* 100 stale) (* threshold n)) 'compact-now)\n          (else 'ok))))\n(define c (hash-table 'a 'stale 'b 'fresh 'c 'stale 'd 'stale))\n(display (list 'at-50 (load-factor-alarm c 50) 'at-90 (load-factor-alarm c 90)))\n(newline)"
     :base "hash-table-count returns how many associations satisfy a two-argument predicate."
     :composition "(define (health-report entities)\n  (list 'alive (hash-table-count entities (lambda (k v) (eq? v 'alive)))\n        'dead (hash-table-count entities (lambda (k v) (eq? v 'dead)))\n        'total (hash-table-size entities)))\n(define e (hash-table 'player 'alive 'goblin 'dead 'bat 'dead))\n(display (health-report e))\n(newline)\n(hash-table-set! e 'player 'dead)\n(display (health-report e))\n(newline)"
     :emergent "a histogram over a table — several counts sharing one collection, each with its own predicate, together forming a summary whose parts are checkable against the whole because they must sum to the size"
     :meta "The composition chains repeated hash-table-count calls, one per bucket, with hash-table-size as the total. What emerges is the summary-statistic family: individually each count is a scalar, but together with a covering set of predicates they become a DISTRIBUTION, and the covering property gives a built-in consistency check that a hand-rolled tally does not have. The same family appears as a Prometheus histogram's bucket set, as SQL's GROUP BY with a COUNT aggregate, as a test runner's pass/fail/skip line. What makes it emergent rather than three separate calls is the invariant across them — the parts must sum to the whole, so the summary can audit itself. The engineering cost is that each bucket is its own linear pass; a single hash-table-fold computing all buckets at once is the optimisation, and it trades the self-auditing shape for speed. See book-of-composition ch09 on aggregate summaries.")
)
)

(verb :name "hash-table-empty?" :std "SRFI-125" :library "hash" :tier "core" :kind "pure" :signature "(hash-table-empty? h) -> boolean"
  :summary "#t when h holds no associations at all, #f otherwise. SRFI-125."
  :explanation "The emptiness predicate. It answers the same question as (= 0 (hash-table-size h)) but says it in the language of the question rather than in arithmetic, and it is the guard the mutating verbs expect you to have asked before you call them — hash-table-pop! on an empty table is an error, and hash-table-empty? is how you avoid it. Because it only asks whether any entry exists, it does not need to look at any key or value."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(hash-table-empty? (make-hash-table))" :note "#t — a fresh table is empty.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(let ((h (hash-table 'a 1)))\n  (list (hash-table-empty? h)\n        (begin (hash-table-delete! h 'a) (hash-table-empty? h))))" :note "(#f #t) — emptiness is a property of the current state, not of how the table was made.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(let ((queue (hash-table 'job-1 'pending 'job-2 'pending)))\n  (let drain ((done (list)))\n    (if (hash-table-empty? queue)\n        (begin (display (reverse done)) (newline))\n        (let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! queue)))\n          (drain (cons k done))))))" :note "(job-1 job-2) — the standard drain loop: test for empty, pop, repeat. Without the test the last pop would raise.")
  )
  :caveats (
    "It is a predicate about the CURRENT state. A table that was just cleared is empty; a table built empty and then written to is not. Nothing about the constructor survives into this answer."
    "Passing a non-table raises rather than returning #t. This is deliberate — a silent #t for a mistyped argument would make a drain loop exit immediately and look like success."
    "It reports whether any association exists, not whether any value is interesting. A table holding one entry bound to #f, 0, or the empty list is NOT empty."
    "Emptiness is not stable across mutation. In a loop that pops or deletes, re-test on every iteration rather than hoisting the call out."
  )
  :usecases ("Guarding hash-table-pop!, which raises on an empty table." "Loop termination for drain-the-table processing." "Short-circuiting a report or a render when there is nothing to show.")
  :related ("hash-table-size" "hash-table-pop!" "hash-table-clear!" "hash-table-count")
  :learn (:concept "A named predicate for the base case of a collection." :prerequisites ("hash-table-size") :progression "Notice first that (= 0 (hash-table-size h)) works; then prefer hash-table-empty? because it names the question; then learn which verbs REQUIRE the guard, starting with hash-table-pop!.")
  :provenance "SRFI-125 §The whole hash table; wired lib/std/srfi-125.js 2026-08-02; reference record 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define inbox (make-hash-table))\n(display (list 'at-boot (hash-table-empty? inbox)))\n(newline)\n(hash-table-set! inbox 'msg-1 'unread)\n(display (list 'after-arrival (hash-table-empty? inbox)))\n(newline)\n(hash-table-clear! inbox)\n(display (list 'after-clear (hash-table-empty? inbox)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define h (make-hash-table))\n(display (list 'domain-is-empty-set (hash-table-empty? h)\n               'no-keys (null? (hash-table-keys h))\n               'cardinality-zero (= 0 (hash-table-size h))\n               'all-three-agree (and (hash-table-empty? h)\n                                     (null? (hash-table-keys h))\n                                     (= 0 (hash-table-size h)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define h (hash-table 'k #f))\n(display (list 'has-one-entry (not (hash-table-empty? h))\n               'whose-value-is-false (eq? #f (hash-table-ref/default h 'k 'MISS))\n               'so-presence-not-truth (not (eq? (hash-table-empty? h)\n                                                (not (hash-table-ref/default h 'k #f))))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator is writing a worker that drains a job table one entry at a time with hash-table-pop!, which raises when there is nothing left. The loop needs a termination test. Three candidates exist. Catching the error from a failed pop would work, but it uses an exception for ordinary control flow and leaves the auditor unable to distinguish 'finished normally' from 'the pop failed for another reason'. Comparing (= 0 (hash-table-size h)) works and is exact, but it asks an arithmetic question to learn a structural fact, and the reader has to confirm the comparison operator and the literal 0 are the intended ones. hash-table-empty? states the termination condition in the same words as the specification of hash-table-pop!'s precondition, so the auditor can match the guard to the requirement by reading, not by reasoning."
     :program "(define queue (hash-table 'job-1 'pending 'job-2 'pending))\n(let drain ((done (list)))\n  (if (hash-table-empty? queue)\n      (begin (display (reverse done)) (newline))\n      (let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! queue)))\n        (drain (cons k done)))))\n(display (hash-table-empty? queue))\n(newline)"
     :explanation "Line 1 builds a two-job queue. Line 2 opens a named-let loop carrying the list of finished job names. Line 3 tests emptiness FIRST, before any pop is attempted — this ordering is the whole safety argument, because hash-table-pop! has an unmet precondition on an empty table. Line 4 is the base case: print what was done. Lines 5-6 pop one association, binding both the key and the value, and recur with the key recorded. Lines 7-8 confirm the queue is empty afterwards, which shows the loop consumed everything rather than stopping early. The audit tape shows the guard evaluated once per iteration including the last, which is why the loop never reaches a pop it cannot satisfy."
     :meta "Why hash-table-empty? and not a size comparison: they compute the same answer, but the guard's job is to be recognisably the same condition as the precondition it protects. Matching names lets the auditor pair them by inspection. Why not catch the pop error: an exception handler around a loop body catches errors the operator did not intend to catch, so a genuine bug inside the body would be silently read as 'queue finished'.")
    (:eng "Test emptiness at the TOP of the loop, not the bottom, so the zero-iteration case is handled by the same code as the last iteration. A bottom-tested drain loop pops once before checking, which is precisely the bug the guard exists to prevent."
     :math "The predicate asks whether dom(f) = ∅, that is whether f is the empty partial function. It is the recogniser for the initial object of the finite-partial-function lattice — the unique table that agrees with every other table on nothing at all."
     :cs "This is the standard is-empty accessor: Python's not d, Java's Map.isEmpty, Clojure's empty?. Its value over a size comparison is intent, and in some data structures also cost — an is-empty check can be O(1) where counting is O(n), so the habit of preferring it transfers to structures where it matters.")
)
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Asking whether a container holds anything at all — a single yes-or-no question that must be answered before any operation that assumes there is something to work on."
     :rungs ((:domain "hash" :code "(define inbox (make-hash-table))\n(display (hash-table-empty? inbox))\n(newline)\n(hash-table-set! inbox 'msg-1 'unread)\n(display (hash-table-empty? inbox))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "an empty mailbox on the porch" :code "(define mailbox (make-hash-table))\n(define (check-mail!)\n  (if (hash-table-empty? mailbox) 'nothing-today 'you-have-mail))\n(display (check-mail!))\n(newline)\n(hash-table-set! mailbox 'letter-1 'from-gran)\n(display (check-mail!))\n(newline)")
             (:domain "the empty set, and the vacuous truth that follows" :code "(define empty (make-hash-table))\n(define nonempty (hash-table 'a 1))\n(display (list 'empty-is-empty (hash-table-empty? empty)\n               'every-member-satisfies-anything (= 0 (hash-table-count empty (lambda (k v) #f)))\n               'and-also-its-negation (= 0 (hash-table-count empty (lambda (k v) #t)))\n               'nonempty-distinguishes (not (= (hash-table-count nonempty (lambda (k v) #t))\n                                               (hash-table-count nonempty (lambda (k v) #f))))))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "In the hash-table domain the predicate reports whether any association exists right now. At the mailbox the question is answered by opening the lid once — the householder does not count the letters to learn there are none, and the answer changes the moment the carrier arrives. In the set domain emptiness is the special case where every universally quantified statement holds vacuously: over the empty table, the always-true predicate and the always-false predicate both count 0, so they become indistinguishable, and it takes a non-empty table to tell them apart. All three share the shape: a base case that is recognised without examining any element, and that makes element-wise questions degenerate."
     :meta "The shape transfers to every collection with a notion of nothing — a null string, a zero-length vector, an empty directory, a queue with no jobs, a result set with no rows. It also transfers to the reasoning ABOUT them: the empty case is where quantifiers collapse, so it is both the easiest case to handle and the easiest case to get wrong, which is why it deserves a named predicate rather than an inline comparison."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define h (make-hash-table))\n(display (list 'fresh (hash-table-empty? h)))\n(newline)\n(hash-table-set! h 'k 1)\n(display (list 'after-set (hash-table-empty? h)))\n(newline)\n(hash-table-delete! h 'k)\n(display (list 'after-delete (hash-table-empty? h)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (agrees-with-size? h)\n  (eq? (hash-table-empty? h) (= 0 (hash-table-size h))))\n(define (agrees-with-keys? h)\n  (eq? (hash-table-empty? h) (null? (hash-table-keys h))))\n(define fresh (make-hash-table))\n(define one (hash-table 'a 1))\n(define many (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3))\n(define cleared (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2))\n(hash-table-clear! cleared)\n(display (list 'size-agrees (map agrees-with-size? (list fresh one many cleared))\n               'keys-agree (map agrees-with-keys? (list fresh one many cleared))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define h (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3))\n(define (pop-until-empty t)\n  (let loop ((n 0))\n    (if (hash-table-empty? t)\n        n\n        (let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! t)))\n          (loop (+ n 1))))))\n(define popped (pop-until-empty h))\n(display (list 'popped popped 'now-empty (hash-table-empty? h)))\n(newline)"
     :claim "hash-table-empty? is true exactly when the size is 0 and exactly when the key list is null; it flips to #f on the first insertion and back to #t when the last association is removed, however it is removed."
     :program "(define h (make-hash-table))\n(display (list 'fresh (hash-table-empty? h) (= 0 (hash-table-size h))))\n(newline)\n(hash-table-set! h 'a 1)\n(hash-table-set! h 'b 2)\n(display (list 'two-entries (hash-table-empty? h) (= 0 (hash-table-size h))))\n(newline)\n(hash-table-delete! h 'a)\n(display (list 'one-left (hash-table-empty? h) (= 0 (hash-table-size h))))\n(newline)\n(hash-table-clear! h)\n(display (list 'cleared (hash-table-empty? h) (= 0 (hash-table-size h))))\n(newline)"
     :invariant "∀ h. empty?(h) ⟺ size(h) = 0 ⟺ keys(h) = () ∧ after set!(h, k, v). ¬empty?(h) ∧ after clear!(h). empty?(h)"
     :meta "The pattern is redundant-observer-agreement: three independent observers of the same underlying state — a predicate, a count, and a list — must never disagree. That mutual agreement is what makes any one of them trustworthy, and it is cheap to test at every state transition. What would falsify: emptiness true while a key is listed, emptiness surviving an insertion, or a cleared table still reporting non-empty. The delete path and the clear path are tested separately on purpose, because an implementation can maintain a size counter correctly in one and not the other. See book-of-code ch09 on invariants across state transitions."
     :eng "The engineering payoff is that emptiness, size and the key list can be used interchangeably in reasoning, so a proof written in terms of one carries over to code written in terms of another."
     :math "The claim is that the emptiness predicate is the recogniser for dom(f) = ∅, and the equivalences are the standard characterisations of the empty set by cardinality and by enumeration. The transition clauses state that insertion strictly grows the domain and clearing collapses it."
     :cs "This is the accessor-consistency contract for a container: is-empty, size, and iteration must all be derived from the same state. Violations are classic bugs in structures that cache a size field separately from the storage.")
)
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define queue (hash-table 'job-1 'render 'job-2 'save 'job-3 'upload))\n(define (run-until-drained! q)\n  (let loop ((log (list)))\n    (if (hash-table-empty? q)\n        (reverse log)\n        (let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! q)))\n          (loop (cons (list k 'did v) log))))))\n(display (run-until-drained! queue))\n(newline)\n(display (list 'queue-empty (hash-table-empty? queue)))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (fixed-point-reached? before after)\n  (and (hash-table-empty? after) (not (hash-table-empty? before))))\n(define work (hash-table 'a 3 'b 1 'c 2))\n(define (step! t)\n  (hash-table-for-each (lambda (k v) (hash-table-set! t k (- v 1))) t)\n  (hash-table-prune! (lambda (k v) (<= v 0)) t))\n(let loop ((rounds 0))\n  (if (hash-table-empty? work)\n      (begin (display (list 'rounds-to-fixed-point rounds)) (newline))\n      (begin (step! work) (loop (+ rounds 1)))))"
     :cs-composition "(define (retry-with-backlog! backlog attempt)\n  (let loop ((round 0) (results (list)))\n    (if (or (hash-table-empty? backlog) (> round 3))\n        (list 'rounds round 'results (reverse results) 'left (hash-table-size backlog))\n        (let ((done (list)))\n          (hash-table-for-each\n            (lambda (k v) (if (attempt k v) (set! done (cons k done))))\n            backlog)\n          (for-each (lambda (k) (hash-table-delete! backlog k)) done)\n          (loop (+ round 1) (cons (length done) results))))))\n(define b (hash-table 'a 1 'b 2 'c 3 'd 4))\n(display (retry-with-backlog! b (lambda (k v) (even? v))))\n(newline)"
     :base "hash-table-empty? reports whether a table currently holds no associations."
     :composition "(define queue (hash-table 'job-1 'render 'job-2 'save 'job-3 'upload))\n(define (run-until-drained! q)\n  (let loop ((log (list)))\n    (if (hash-table-empty? q)\n        (reverse log)\n        (let-values (((k v) (hash-table-pop! q)))\n          (loop (cons (list k 'did v) log))))))\n(display (run-until-drained! queue))\n(newline)\n(display (hash-table-empty? queue))\n(newline)"
     :emergent "the drain loop — a self-terminating consumer whose stopping condition is the container's own emptiness, so the number of iterations is data, not a constant the programmer has to keep in sync"
     :meta "The composition chains hash-table-empty? (the guard) with hash-table-pop! (the consumer that shrinks the table) inside a recursion. What emerges is the worklist family: termination is guaranteed not by counting but by the structural fact that each iteration strictly reduces the size and the guard recognises zero. That argument is a well-founded induction, and it is why a drain loop is safe even when the body ADDS entries — as long as it removes more than it adds. The same family appears as a message-queue consumer, as a graph traversal's frontier loop, as a garbage collector's mark stack, as make's pending-target list. What makes it emergent rather than a plain predicate call is the coupling: the guard and the consumer must agree about what emptiness means, and the mutating verb's precondition is exactly the guard's negation. See book-of-composition ch10 on worklist algorithms and book-of-math ch05 on well-founded recursion.")
)
)
